node_modules/
# A BROWSER PROFILE, whatever it is called. `testing/profile/` is the shared harness's; a lane driving a
# second browser concurrently must give its own a different name to avoid colliding with it, and that
# isolation is correct — so the pattern matches the SHAPE rather than the one name, or every such lane
# leaves 20-odd MB of Chrome state looking like source.
testing/profile*/
testing/reports/
extension.crx
testing/harness-dumps
testing/finding-snapshots
# THE SERVED FIXTURES. Ignored WHOLE, with no reasoning beside it, in a block of scratch and output — and
# this file's own paragraph 20 lines down argues the opposite for the same kind of artifact. THE JUSTIFICATION
# THAT USED TO STAND HERE WAS A QUOTATION OF CLAUDE.md §Testing requiring a before/after on FROZEN BYTES,
# "a mirror, a fixture, a recorded payload replayed" — and the project owner has RETIRED the mirror half of
# that sentence: this repository carries no copy of anybody else's site, because a page's scripts and styles
# are computed and fetched at runtime. THE ARGUMENT FOR TRACKING OUR OWN FIXTURES IS UNTOUCHED BY THAT AND
# NEVER DEPENDED ON IT: a fixture this project WROTE is an INSTRUMENT, not a capture of a third party, and
# "Bytes that are not committed cannot be frozen HERE" is a fact about the clone rather than about whose
# bytes they are. A gate runs
# from a `git clone --shared` snapshot, and a clone brings nothing git does not track, so an ignored fixture is
# one no frozen run can serve and one a fresh container does not have. Most of what is in this directory really
# is scratch — a lane's throwaway page, a generated body — so the directory stays ignored and a fixture that is
# an INSTRUMENT is re-included BY NAME. The pattern is `/*` rather than the bare directory because git cannot
# re-include a path whose parent directory is excluded; excluding the entries has the same effect and leaves the
# negation reachable.
testing/fixtures/*
# The §8.1.4.2 script-status conformance fixture and the two bodies it serves. It asserts a spec-defined
# observable of an unmodified page (a non-ok `<script src>` fires `error`, not `load`, and its body never
# becomes a program), so it is not one run's scratch — it is the artifact a before/after is taken ON.
!testing/fixtures/scrstat_*
# THE css-transforms-1 §7 GRAMMAR FALSIFIER. Re-included by name for the reason the paragraph above gives and
# for one more that is specific to it: it is a document whose whole content is a PREDICTION, so the run that
# scores it is one somebody else makes, at a revision somebody else names, and quotes back. A prediction whose
# document is not in the revision it was predicted about cannot be re-run to check the quote — which is the
# shape CLAUDE.md §Testing calls a measurement that outlived its instrument, where the number was true, the
# method was sound, and only the thing that produced it is gone. Its three arms are stated at the arms
# themselves, and one of them is a REGRESSION GUARD rather than a gap: `transform: translate(2)` must compute
# `none` and not abort, which is the page-held abort switch 0b5c14d0 removed from core/css.
!testing/fixtures/tf_grammar.html
# THE STAGE LADDER FOR THE LOST `transform` DECLARATION, re-included by name for the same reason its sibling
# above is: it is a document whose content is an EXPERIMENT, and one somebody else runs at a revision somebody
# else names. Its `color` control is declared in the same attribute as its subject, so a run of it is only
# readable against the revision it was written for.
!testing/fixtures/tf_decl_probe.html
# THE FIVE-FIXTURE PARTITION FOR AN ATTRIBUTE SELECTOR DECIDED FROM AN UNKNOWN, re-included by name
# because the five are ONE EXPERIMENT and each differs from its neighbours on ONE axis: whether the value
# behind `[data-theme=...]` is a LITERAL, an UNKNOWN NOBODY PINNED, or an UNKNOWN THE FLOW WENT ON TO PIN —
# and, among the pinned ones, whether the page READ THE PROPERTY TWICE or once into a shared variable, and
# whether the equality that pins runs BEFORE or AFTER the store. A partition is worth nothing unless every
# arm is in the revision the others were measured at, because the whole reading is a COMPARISON between them:
# a run of one arm alone answers nothing, and an arm that is not in the clone cannot be served by a frozen
# gate, which is the only place these numbers are quotable.
# `themepin_literal` is the POSITIVE CONTROL and is the load-bearing one: it contains no unknown at all, so
# a run of it that paints #00ff00 rather than #101011 says the cascade does not re-run over an attribute
# selector after `setAttribute` AT ALL — and every zero the other four report is then a statement about that
# and not about a pin. `themepin_unpinned` is the second control, and the one that arms the SEAM: nothing
# ever pins its value, so the abort must SPEAK there. CLAUDE.md
# §A-CONTROL-ARMS-ONLY-ON-A-SITE-THE-INSTRUMENT-CAN-JUDGE: a probe that has never made its check speak has
# calibrated nothing, and the failure direction is the expensive one, since this file's prescribed response
# to a real blind spot is to go and build an instrument for it.
# MEASURED at ed034f57, five drives of the served fixtures through the shipped ABI, so the two controls are
# not a hope: `literal` paints TWO worlds whose centre pixels are `00 ff 00` and `10 10 11`, and `unpinned`
# fires the seam 1 with its negative control at 0. The three pinned arms are BYTE-IDENTICAL to each other —
# same two worlds, same seam count, same terminal — which is what retires the read-count and the ordering as
# explanations and leaves the pin read itself as the subject.
# AND THE FAMILY GAINED A PAIR FOR (1) THREE-VALUED MATCHING, ALREADY COVERED BY THE PATTERN ABOVE AND
# NAMED HERE BECAUSE THE PATTERN IS NOT THE REASONING. `themepin_triunknown` is the SUBJECT: no equality
# appears in it at all, so every flow that stores the attribute reaches the cascade having determined
# nothing, and it isolates the one population the five arms above have to reason around. `themepin_trior` is
# its ARMED CONTROL and is that document with the two rules written as ONE SELECTOR LIST — a branch that
# matches under every value the attribute could hold, so Selectors 4 §17.3's answer is a definite TRUE while
# the value seam is still asked. The two differ in exactly one property of the selector and in nothing about
# the value, the flow or the seam, which is what lets them tell a DECISION from a relabelled crash: before
# (1) both abort and are indistinguishable, after it the control completes and the subject's abort MOVES.
# BOTH ASK THE CASCADE THROUGH `getComputedStyle` RATHER THAN THROUGH A PAINT, which is why they are
# quotable from a terminal event alone: the five arms above reach Selectors 4 §17.3 by rendering, so a drive
# without a raster answers nothing about them, and CSSOM §6.1 is the same cascade with no raster in it.
# A control that has never made its check speak has calibrated nothing (CLAUDE.md
# §A-CONTROL-ARMS-ONLY-ON-A-SITE-THE-INSTRUMENT-CAN-JUDGE), and `themepin_trior` fires the OLD seam abort at
# the revisions before (1) — which is what makes its silence afterwards a result rather than an unarmed probe.
!testing/fixtures/themepin_*.html
# THE VALUES-BACKING READBACK PAIR, re-included by name because the two are ONE EXPERIMENT and the
# silent one is worthless without the loud one. `7c4c6b3f` moved every declaration block's READ onto a
# values store with the serialization kept as a projection, and named its own biggest unmeasured risk:
# an ORDINARY block -- no unknown anywhere -- must still answer `color`, `length` and `cssText` exactly
# as before. Both fixtures are that block. `cssd_readback_ok` asserts the CORRECT values and throws on a
# mismatch, so its SILENCE is the result; `cssd_readback_fail` is identical except that it asserts a
# value that is deliberately wrong, so it MUST throw. CLAUDE.md
# §A-CONTROL-ARMS-ONLY-ON-A-SITE-THE-INSTRUMENT-CAN-JUDGE: a probe that has never made its check speak
# has calibrated nothing, and an absent crash is not a correct value.
# MEASURED at 7c4c6b3f through the shipped ABI: the control fires and its message carries `color=red`,
# which is POSITIVE evidence of the value rather than an absence, and the subject is silent -- so the
# store answers `red`, `5` and a `cssText` containing `color: red`.
!testing/fixtures/cssd_readback_*.html
# THE `webpackJsonp` PRESENT/ABSENT PAIR AND THE FOUR BODIES IT LOADS. THREE TRACKED FILES QUOTE NUMBERS
# MEASURED ON THIS DOCUMENT AND NAME IT BY PATH, and the bytes were in no revision: engine/host/solver/flow.c's
# argument for why `rankChanges` is not the arrival count (34315 against 34309 at the last of twelve samples,
# a 180 s series at artifact c23bfe6a), engine/host/solver/flow.h's worked example off that same series, and
# testing/live-run.js's `scheduler-busy-before-this-run` arm, which says in its own words "it is why this arm
# exists" over six interleaved runs at artifact 00754e96. CLAUDE.md §Testing: "an instrument whose output
# anyone quotes is COMMITTED, in the same diff as the first quotation of its number", the tell being that you
# can state a number and cannot state the PATH that produces it. Here the path WAS stated, four times, in two
# engine sources carrying landed design arguments — so a fresh clone could read the argument, read the
# document's name, and reproduce nothing. That is the one shape this file's paragraph above is about.
# THE CLOSURE IS THE FIXTURE, NOT THE DOCUMENT. wjp_absent.html is four `<script src>`s and wjp_entry.js
# fetches /wjp_probe.json; the server resolves those by constructed path, so a page committed without them is
# a different document that happens to share a name. `_present` is the other arm — the same bytes plus
# `window.webpackJsonp=[]`, which is the whole question the family asks — and it is here on the ground the
# scrstat block above argues at length: a control is load-bearing, because an engine that answers nothing
# leaves the same absence a correct answer leaves. It is 295 bytes and no tracked file names it; the four
# sub-resources are webpack-shaped bodies this fixture is built out of, not a site mirror, so they carry no
# testing/corpus/provenance.json row and are not owed one.
!testing/fixtures/wjp_*
# THE STYLE-ORIGIN PARTITION, FIVE DOCUMENTS THAT DIFFER IN ONE AXIS AND NOTHING ELSE. Each declares the same
# 400x200 box in the same colour and they differ only in WHERE the declaration comes from: a script setting the
# attribute (`scriptmark`), a static `style=` attribute (`inlinemark`), an internal `<style>` sheet
# (`sheetmark`), an external `<link>` sheet (`linkmark` plus its one `.css`), and an internal sheet whose
# selectors are ESCAPED (`escmark`). They are re-included by name for the reason the scrstat block above gives
# and for one that is specific to the set: EVERY paint this project has shown came from a script setting a style
# attribute, which demonstrates nothing about whether an AUTHOR sheet reaches the cascade at all, and a
# partition is the only thing that can separate the two. A frame's mark count over these five says which origin
# stops reaching the painter, where one document says only that something did.
# `escmark` IS A REGRESSION GUARD AND NOT A GAP, and it is the member that has to be in a revision. c388ccbb's
# whole scale argument is that it-tools.tech's 34 KB author sheet carries 109 escaped selectors — re-derived
# here as 92 `\:`, 6 `\%`, 3 `\#`, 3 `\!`, 2 `\/`, 2 `\.` and 1 `\>` — which is what a utility-CSS
# generator emits, and before that commit a selector's serialization dropped every escape, so the author
# cascade re-parsed `.sm\:w-1\/2` as `.sm:w-1/2`, which is not a selector: lexbor destroyed the style rule
# and put a BAD_STYLE rule at its index. No fixture in this tree exercised an escape, so nothing but a live
# page could see it. This one carries `\!` and `\:`, the two commonest shapes in that census.
!testing/fixtures/scriptmark.html
!testing/fixtures/inlinemark.html
!testing/fixtures/sheetmark.html
!testing/fixtures/linkmark.html
!testing/fixtures/linkmark.css
!testing/fixtures/escmark.html
# THE §6.6 BACKING PAIR — the before/after instrument for a CSS declaration block that keeps its
# declarations as VALUES rather than as their serialization, re-included by name for the reason the paragraph
# at the top of this block gives. The two are ONE instrument and neither is readable without the other: they
# differ in exactly one declaration (`margin: var(--g) 0` against `margin: 7px 0`), they make the identical
# write, and both end at a §6.6.1 write of an unknown with NO EXAMPLE — so the CONCRETE arm's abort is the
# REACHABILITY WITNESS that says the mixed arm's earlier abort was a difference in the engine and not a
# statement that never ran. Measured 3 of 3 on each arm at 0ea57e5a. They are also the document a standing
# prediction is scored on, which is the shape CLAUDE.md §Testing calls a measurement that outlived its
# instrument: a prediction whose document is not in the revision it was predicted about cannot be re-run.
!testing/fixtures/cssdback_m5ey_mixed.html
!testing/fixtures/cssdback_m5ey_concrete.html
testing/*.lock
# DOT-PREFIXED SCRATCH under testing/ — a run's captured stdout, a recorded trace, a generated
# fixture body. Regenerated by the next run and meaningless without the run that wrote it, and the
# dot prefix is already how they say so. This rule is here for a reason the 887 tracked build logs
# under engine/.work made expensive: an ignore added AFTER a file is tracked does not apply to it, so
# scratch that reaches the index once stays there being rewritten by every subsequent run, dirtying a
# tree several agents share and putting a stray path in reach of somebody else's commit. Ignore it
# before it is ever added, not after.
testing/.*.out
testing/.*.jsonl
testing/.*.json
testing/profile-lane/
# A LIVE RUN'S CAPTURED OUTPUT under testing/corpus/. The rule above states the principle and keys it on a
# dot prefix, which a lane writing testing/corpus/logs/<site>.log does not carry — so the principle held and
# the pattern did not. The shape is what makes these scratch, and for a LIVE site it is stronger than for a
# fixture: CLAUDE.md §Testing says one run of a live site is not a measurement, so a raw per-run record is
# meaningless without the run that wrote it AND without the spread it is one sample of. What survives a live
# run is the distilled report — the number with its run count, its spread, and the revision it was measured
# at — not the transcript. Anything here that deserves to last is quoted somewhere that is not this directory.
testing/corpus/logs/
testing/corpus/census-*.jsonl
# THIS BLOCK USED TO SAY `testing/corpus/mirror/` AND `provenance.json` ARE A RUN'S INPUT AND ARE COMMITTED,
# AND THE PROJECT OWNER HAS RETIRED THAT. It is REWRITTEN rather than deleted because its argument is sound
# and a reader who re-derives it will re-introduce the mirror: CLAUDE.md §Testing wanted a before/after on
# FROZEN BYTES, and bytes that are not committed cannot be frozen HERE, since a frozen snapshot is a
# `git clone --shared` and a clone brings nothing git does not track — so an ignored capture dies with the
# container and the next run re-fetches a site that has moved under it. What that reasoning gets wrong is the
# SUBJECT rather than the mechanism, on two counts. A page's scripts and styles are COMPUTED AND FETCHED AT
# RUNTIME, so a frozen copy of somebody's app is a program no visitor is served and an engine measured
# against one is measured against nothing anybody runs. And other people's bundles are not this project's to
# commit at all, which is a decision and not a trade-off. testing/corpus/README.md is where it is recorded.
# WHAT REPLACES IT IS A TRACKED DRIVER AND AN IGNORED OUTPUT, which is the shape this file already uses for
# every corpus fetched at run time (V8's mjsunit and the WPT checkout below). A fresh clone gets the list of
# ADDRESSES and the code that visits them; it never gets the site. The repeatability the capture bought is
# bought instead by what §Testing prescribes for a moving subject — a run COUNT and a SPREAD rather than one
# number, compared on what does not move with reach — and by the manifest recording the (url, date, sha256)
# triple for every response, so a figure read out of one run is a fact about the instant that run names.
# THE FETCHED SITE CORPUS: testing/corpus/fetch.mjs's output, and the one input engine/absentrank.mjs and
# engine/nsguardrank.mjs take. It is third-party minified JavaScript, it is large, and it is somebody else's.
engine/.work/sitecorpus/
# COW build artifact: cow-barrier-instrumented wasm base64 blob (~38MB), regenerable via
# `node engine/build.mjs cow`. Never committed — production ships the SINGLE_FILE qjs_worker.js.
extension/lib/qjs/*.cow.gen.js
# v2 engine build outputs staged into the extension by `node engine/build.mjs` (source of truth =
# engine/host/main.c + the engine/qjs submodule). Regenerable; not committed to avoid binary churn.
extension/lib/qjs/qjs.mjs
extension/lib/qjs/qjs.wasm
# The BROWSER PROCESS's program, linked by the same build out of its own source list
# (engine/host/browser_process/* + core/mime/mime_type.c) and staged where the offscreen's Worker loads it.
# Regenerable build output, ignored for the same churn reason as the renderer's above.
extension/lib/bproc/bproc.mjs
extension/lib/bproc/bproc.wasm
# THE ARTIFACT'S BUILD STAMP — the revision `build.mjs` linked it from, written through gate_revision.mjs
# so the stamp and the check are one answer. Output beside the output it describes, and ignored for the
# same reason: committing it would pin an identity to a tree that no longer has the artifact it names.
extension/lib/qjs/qjs.mjs.build.json
# engine/host/out/ IS BUILD OUTPUT, ALL OF IT. This said the opposite — "qjs.js and qjs.wasm are the shipped
# artifacts and are committed" — and build.mjs:110 states in its own words that they are not: `link()` emits
# `test_forced.c -> out/qjs.js` and `main.c -> extension/lib/qjs/qjs.mjs`, and the second one is the shipped
# ABI entry, gitignored six lines up for churn. out/qjs.js is the SMOKE program. So a 5.8 MB binary was tracked
# under a sentence describing a different file, and nobody regenerated it: it was last rebuilt at 1da7a400 with
# 203 later commits touching engine/host and engine/qjs. A committed artifact nobody refreshes is worse than no
# artifact, because it reads as authoritative.
# AND IT MADE EVERY FROZEN SNAPSHOT DIRTY BY CONSTRUCTION. §Testing requires a gate to run from a snapshot and
# report the revision it measured; engine/gate_revision.mjs now does. Its first run said "THE COMPILED CONE IS
# DIRTY — engine/host/out/qjs.js, engine/host/out/qjs.wasm" — because the build had just written them. A
# tracked file the build overwrites means no gate run can ever quote a clean revision, which defeats the
# mechanism rather than inconveniencing it. Untracked, the whole directory is output and the cone stays clean.
engine/host/out/
# native-ASan toolchain + build artifacts (node engine/asan.mjs) — large, regenerated/downloaded on demand
engine/.work/llvm-mingw-extract/
engine/.work/llvm-mingw.zip
engine/.work/lxobj/
# The per-source object cache `node engine/build.mjs` compiles into (one .o + .d per translation unit,
# rebuilt whenever the source or any header it recorded is newer). Regenerable build output, not source.
engine/.work/obj/
# A gate's own output is not source: a corpus run writes its transcript beside the build
# artifacts, and it belongs to the run rather than to the repository.
engine/.work/*.log
engine/.work/lxobj_clang/
# The emscripten lexbor build products. Every sibling artifact here was already ignored; these two were not,
# and a COMMITTED liblexbor.o is a live trap: a container that provisions a different emcc links its fresh
# headers against a stale archive and the engine takes a "memory access out of bounds" with nothing in the
# source to explain it. build.mjs compiles it when missing, which is the only correct source for it.
engine/.work/liblexbor.o
engine/.work/lexbor.rsp
engine/.work/liblexbor_asan.a
engine/.work/liblexbor_clang.a
engine/.work/test_asan.exe
engine/.recursion-ir/
# V8's mjsunit corpus, FETCHED at run time by engine/v8mjsunit.mjs, plus the harness directory and the
# per-file scratch each run produces. The harness says nothing is vendored; twenty of V8's test files had
# been committed under the old corpus's work directory, which made that false. They are gone and the
# directory is ignored, so it stays true.
engine/.work/v8regexp/
engine/.work/v8mjsunit/
# DELETED: engine/.work/lexbor-src/. lexbor is no longer fetched — its source is TRACKED at engine/lexbor,
# vendored at v3.0.0 with the fork's delta as the next commit. "Regenerable; never committed" was true of a
# pristine checkout and became false the moment tree construction needed an embedder seam: the delta lived only
# in a working tree that a fresh container re-cloned away, and the build's tag check passed anyway because a
# working-tree edit leaves HEAD on the tag.
engine/.work/liblexbor.srcid
engine/.work/lxnat/
engine/.work/lxlist
# The same lexbor, built natively by the WPT gate (cmake + make into a static archive) so the gate's runner can
# link the real DOM. Built on first use from the checkout above; regenerable, never committed.
engine/.work/lexbor-native/

# Scratch CLONES under .work — toolchains and side experiments (emsdk, depot_tools, z3, qemu-wasm,
# unicorn-js, mini-rv32ima). Each is its own git repo, so `git add -A` recorded it as a GITLINK with no
# .gitmodules entry, which makes `git submodule status` fatal for every clone. They are fetched, not vendored.
engine/.work/depot_tools/
engine/.work/emsdk/
engine/.work/mini-rv32ima/
engine/.work/qemu-wasm/
engine/.work/unicorn-js/
engine/.work/z3/
# the Web Platform Tests checkout (engine/wpt.mjs) — the browser half's oracle, cloned on demand like the corpus
engine/.work/wpt/
engine/.work/wpt-gaps.json

# A LANE'S FROZEN WORKTREE, PARKED INSIDE THE REPO. `git worktree add` anywhere under the checkout leaves a
# full tree git reports as untracked — 111MB when this was written — so one `git add -A` stages the whole
# thing, and CLAUDE.md's ban on `git clean` in a shared checkout means nobody may tidy it away either. Ignored
# rather than removed: a worktree here is registered in .git/worktrees and an agent may be working in it.
# Put new ones in the scratchpad; this entry exists so the ones already here cannot be committed by accident.
base/
